Assets retain their individual LICENSE files, including the H1/G1,
Shadow Hand, Robotiq and kitchen model licenses. The build copies assets
from the pinned Python package without importing its runtime task logic.
The kitchen kettle defaults are nested under an unnamed default to meet
the current MuJoCo XML schema; their numerical values are unchanged.

The local Linux wheel bundles the official MuJoCo 3.6.0 engine and its
OBJ, STL, and touch-grid plugins, preserving their license notices.
Using the same official physics binary avoids compiler-dependent drift
in contact-rich trajectory comparisons. No Python task implementation
is imported or called while running the native environments.
